          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "Adherence means sticking with a treatment plan: taking medicines as prescribed and following the self-care steps agreed with your clinician. The retrieved passages don't give a formal definition, so I can't add more detail from these sources.\n\nDiabetes is largely self-managed. Most of the decisions that affect how it goes are made between appointments. The guidance in *Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review* says adherence to glucose-lowering medication is often imperfect in practice. It lists cost, complexity, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting as common reasons. It also says that treating poor adherence as a character flaw misses these causes, and that each cause has a different fix.\n\nRelated guidance points the same way. The hypertension guidance (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*) notes that apparent treatment resistance is more often caused by measurement problems, poor adherence, or interfering drugs than by a need for more medicine. The heart guidance (*Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis*) notes that adherence tends to fall in the year after a cardiac event, so regular reviews are a chance to check it.\n\nThe sources don't explain why adherence affects blood sugar control or long-term outcomes in detail. If you take diabetes medicine and find it hard to keep up, a clinician or diabetes educator can help you find the cause and a practical fix.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
